WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
The reference architecture.

2
00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:05.507
Northwind’s assistant (fictional, but typical) has these parts.

3
00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:09.947
A web chat UI that renders the assistant’s answers as Markdown.

4
00:00:10.227 --> 00:00:16.029
An API service that authenticates the customer, runs lesson 10’s guards, and calls the model.

5
00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:22.795
A retrieval index over help-center articles, some written by staff and some imported from a community forum.

6
00:00:23.075 --> 00:00:26.808
A booking tool that reads reservations from the company database.

7
00:00:27.088 --> 00:00:33.616
An MCP server for the ticketing system, installed from a public package, that can create and update tickets.

8
00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:36.755
Traces and logs in an observability service.

9
00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:39.808
And the model provider, reached with an API key.

10
00:00:40.088 --> 00:00:50.115
Draw it as a data flow diagram and mark the trust boundaries, the lines where data crosses from something you control to something you don’t, or between privilege levels.

11
00:00:50.395 --> 00:00:59.333
In an LLM system there is one boundary that classic diagrams miss: every place where untrusted text enters the model’s context.

12
00:00:59.613 --> 00:01:01.341
The customer’s message is one.

13
00:01:01.621 --> 00:01:03.584
Retrieved forum posts are another.

14
00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:08.045
Tool results, ticket contents, and anything fetched from the web are more.

15
00:01:08.325 --> 00:01:14.576
The model can’t reliably tell instructions from data, so each of these is an entry point for instructions.
